  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>In complete cation electrolysis at cathode, the species having higher reduction potential is preferably reduced at cathode<br /><math> <mrow> <msubsup> <mtext>E</mtext> <mtext>C</mtext> <mo>&#8728;</mo> </msubsup> <mo>&#62;</mo> <msubsup> <mtext>E</mtext> <mtext>B</mtext> <mo>&#8728;</mo> </msubsup> <mo>&#62;</mo> <msubsup> <mtext>E</mtext> <mtext>D</mtext> <mo>&#8728;</mo> </msubsup> <mo>&#62;</mo> <msubsup> <mtext>E</mtext> <mtext>A</mtext> <mo>&#8728;</mo> </msubsup> </mrow> </math><br />Hence,&nbsp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math><br />will be the preference of deposition of these metals on competing.</p></blockquote>",
